Compartment Syndrome
Acute Urgent Care
Pain out of proportion, Pain on passive stretch, Tense/woody compartment, Pressure >30 mmHg

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-025
Reason for Visit: Unbearable, escalating pain in the lower leg following a cast application earlier today.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted agonizing leg pain that was rapidly worsening despite strong painkillers, occurring hours after a tibial fracture was casted at a local clinic. This is a hallmark presentation of compartment syndrome. Urgent, immediate face-to-face assessment was critical to evaluate tissue pressure and perfusion; delaying until morning would result in irreversible muscle necrosis and nerve death, leading to permanent limb amputation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My leg feels like it is exploding inside the cast, and the pain is a million times worse than when I actually broke the bone!
History of Present Illness: The patient sustained a closed tibial fracture earlier today and was placed in a rigid fiberglass cast. Over the past 4 hours, the patient has developed exquisite pain that is entirely out of proportion to the apparent injury. The pain is unremitting and completely refractory to prescribed oral oxycodone. The patient reports that any attempt to move the toes causes an excruciating exacerbation of the pain (pain on passive stretch). The patient describes a deep, aching, tight sensation, and has recently noticed paraesthesia (pins and needles) over the top of the foot.
Functional Status: Lying on the couch, writhing and screaming in agony.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of permanent limb loss.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 | HR: 115 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Patient is in agonizing pain, crying, and highly distressed.
Physical Exam: The visible portions of the leg above the cast appear shiny, tight, and swollen. The overlying skin is pale. Crucially, passive dorsiflexion and plantarflexion of the toes elicit screaming pain. Capillary refill time in the toes is delayed (4 seconds). Distal peripheral pulses (dorsalis pedis) remain weakly palpable, consistent with the fact that pulses remain intact until the very late stages of the syndrome. There is early paresis (weakness) in toe extension. Expected investigation results involve intracompartmental tissue pressure exceeding 30 mmHg measured via direct needle manometry in the emergency department.
Environmental Observations: The cast is rigid and unyielding; immediate tools for cast bivalving are required.
Medication Reconciliation: Took Oxycodone 10mg 1 hour ago with zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Compartment Syndrome - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Normal Fracture Pain - Ruled out/unlikely due to the pain being "out of proportion," completely refractory to strong opioids, and the severe pain elicited on passive stretch, which are classic signs of ischemia rather than simple bone pain.

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the acute timeline (hours post-injury/casting) and the agonizing severity of the pain with nerve involvement, distinguishing it from the slower onset of DVT.

Acute Arterial Occlusion - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of palpable distal pulses (arterial flow is maintained until compartment pressures exceed arterial pressure, whereas primary occlusion loses pulses immediately).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic, catastrophic signs of acute compartment syndrome: pain out of proportion to the injury, severe pain on passive stretch of the ischemic muscles, and early neurological deficits (paraesthesia and paresis). The rigid cast is acting as a tourniquet over swelling muscle compartments. An urgent home assessment was absolutely life-saving. Without immediate identification and emergency transfer for a decompressive fasciotomy, the muscular and nervous tissue in the anterior compartment would undergo irreversible necrosis within hours, guaranteeing severe permanent disability or limb amputation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediately elevated the leg slightly (no higher than the heart to maintain arterial inflow). Administered Morphine 5mg subcutaneously for severe distress. Called 000 for Priority 1 ambulance transport to an Emergency Department with orthopedic surgical capabilities.
Positive Behavioral Change: By never ignoring rapidly worsening pain under a cast and seeking immediate medical help, you ensure that dangerous pressure can be released before it permanently crushes the nerves and muscles in your leg.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Ambulance transport.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to orthopedic surgery team for emergency fasciotomy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched. The parent is instructed not to elevate the leg highly, as gravity will further reduce the arterial pressure needed to push blood into the pressurized compartment. Keep the leg level with the body.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The pressure inside your leg muscles is dangerously high and is cutting off the blood supply. You need an emergency procedure at the hospital to cut the cast off and likely open the skin to release the pressure.
Clinical Course: If the pressure is released surgically (fasciotomy) within a few hours, the muscles will survive and recover. Delaying surgery leads to permanent muscle death.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to walk or stand on the leg. Try to keep your toes as still as possible to avoid triggering the pain. We are getting the ambulance here immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your broken bone is bleeding and swelling inside the tight muscle sac, but the cast will not let it expand. This is crushing the nerves. The hospital will cut the cast open immediately upon your arrival.
Actionable Steps: Keep your leg resting flat on the couch. Focus on deep breathing to manage the pain while we wait for the paramedics.

